course: dictionary
lesson: 15757
title: Trivial: small and not important
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words sound big but mean something small. Trivial is one of them. Something trivial is small and not important, so it is not worth worrying about. «We argued for an hour about a trivial detail: the colour of the pen.» The argument went on for ages, but the thing they argued about was tiny, so trivial means small and unimportant.
explain: Check your meaning by swapping it into the sentence. Does «a small, unimportant detail» still make sense? Yes, so you have it right. Trivial can also describe a whole matter: «Losing one sock is a trivial matter.»
explain: In maths, trivial means the simplest, most obvious answer, the one that needs no real work. «The equation x × 0 = 0 has a trivial solution: x = 0.» Here trivial does not mean unimportant; it means obvious. Read the words around it to see which meaning the author wants.
explain: Quizzes use trivia. «Trivial facts» are odd little facts that are fun to know but not important. «Question 4 asked about the colour of a famous hat» is a trivial fact in a quiz, because knowing it does not change your life. Context clues tell you which meaning is right.
fact: Trivial means small and not important, not worth worrying about.
fact: In maths, a trivial answer or case is the simplest and most obvious one, needing no real work.
fact: Trivia means odd little facts that are fun to know but not important.
fact: «Trivialise» means to make something serious seem small.
fact: Phrases you will meet: «a trivial matter», «trivial details».
exercise: «We spent ages arguing about a trivial detail, like the colour of the lid.» What does trivial mean here? | Hidden under the sea | Huge and serious | Very dangerous | *Small and not important
exercise: In maths, what is a trivial answer? | Written in Roman numerals | Always a fraction | Very hard to find, needing lots of work | *Simple and obvious, needing little work
exercise: Your quiz asks about a famous hat. What is a trivial fact? | *An odd little fact that is fun but not important | A fact written in code | A fact nobody can ever prove | A fact that is vital for staying alive
test: Ben says, «Forgetting my pencil is trivial, not a disaster.» What does trivial mean? | *Small, not worth worrying about | Terrible and serious | Shiny and new | Yellow and round
test: Which word means to make something serious seem small? | Triangle | Trivial | *Trivialise | Trust
exam: «The teacher said, "Do not trivialise the fire drill, it matters."» What does trivialise mean? | *To make something serious seem small | To count things carefully | To make something bigger and louder | To paint something in bright colours
